    "label": "Finishing the twelfth grade",
    "lane": "learning",
    "kind": "institutional-sequence",
    "optional": true,
    "researchRequired": true,
    "researchNote": "A measured distribution of ages at completion of the twelfth grade is needed. NCES Digest of Education Statistics chapter two, and the Census Bureau October Current Population Survey School Enrollment Supplement detailed tables, cross grade attended against single year of age; resolving query: 'Digest of Education Statistics, enrollment by grade attended and single year of age'. The Mini-Digest structural overview describes only how the program is organized, and states in the same paragraph that the high school level may run a variable number of years depending on district structure, so it cannot supply either an age or a measured share.",
    "windowInWords": "the end of the standard secondary school sequence, with some leaving it earlier and some finishing it later",
    "readRef": "/map/credential-decision",
    "status": "editorial",
    "evidence": "insufficient-evidence",
    "lastReviewed": "2026-09-03",
    "analysis": {
      "early": {
        "tends": "Some students reach the end of the secondary sequence sooner than the class around them, through accelerated coursework, an early graduation policy, or a state equivalency test taken young. What tends to follow is that the credential arrives while the legal and financial machinery around it is still keyed to older ages, so signing a lease, drawing student aid on one's own means, and hiring rules all lag the diploma.",
        "costs": [
          "Institutions that ask for a guardian's signature or a parent's income keep asking for them after the credential is in hand.",
          "The cohort that would otherwise be applying, moving and starting work at the same moment is still enrolled."
        ],
        "routes": [
          "Dual enrollment at a community college, which lets coursework continue without a full college admission.",
          "Deferred admission, which most colleges grant in writing and hold open for a stated period.",
          "Registered apprenticeship and paid pre-apprenticeship programs, which set their own entry ages."
        ],
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "window": {
        "tends": "Most people who finish do so at the end of the standard sequence, and the institutions downstream are built around exactly that timing: admission cycles, aid application windows, entry-level hiring, and enlistment all assume an applicant arriving from the previous step. The path is smoothest here because everything else is scheduled to meet it, not because the credential is different.",
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "late": {
        "tends": "Finishing later comes about through repeated coursework, a move between districts, a return after leaving, or an equivalency route taken as an adult. The credential itself is not time-stamped in a way most employers read. What differs is that the scaffolding around it — counselors, application help, a cohort applying together — belongs to the school year rather than to the person, so every next step is arranged individually.",
        "costs": [
          "The supports attached to the school year are gone, so applications, transcripts and deadlines are self-managed.",
          "Some scholarship and grant programs restrict eligibility to applicants entering directly from secondary school."
        ],
        "routes": [
          "Adult education programs run by school districts and community colleges, which prepare candidates for a state equivalency test.",
          "Adult diploma programs, which award the state diploma itself rather than an equivalency.",
          "Community college open admission, which in most states accepts an equivalency credential on the same footing as a diploma.",
          "Federal and state adult education funding, which makes many of these programs free to the student."
        ],
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "interrupted": {
        "tends": "Leaving partway and returning is common enough that states run parallel machinery for it. Credits already earned usually persist on a transcript and transfer, which makes a return shorter than a restart. What is lost is continuity rather than coursework: a district may not re-enroll someone past its compulsory age, so the way back often runs through adult programs rather than the original school.",
        "costs": [
          "Re-enrollment in a traditional high school can be closed once the state compulsory age has passed.",
          "Credits do not always map cleanly across a district or a state line, so some coursework is repeated."
        ],
        "routes": [
          "Requesting an official transcript, which fixes what has already been earned before any re-entry is negotiated.",
          "Adult high school and evening programs, which enroll past the compulsory age.",
          "The state high school equivalency test, which examines subject knowledge without requiring seat time.",
          "Community college ability-to-benefit provisions, a federal route into aid-eligible coursework for applicants without a secondary credential."
        ],
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "alternative": {
        "tends": "Equivalency credentials, adult diplomas, competency-based programs, and career and technical certificates all reach the place the diploma reaches — evidence of secondary learning that later institutions accept — by different means. Which one fits depends on where it is going next, because the rule sits with the receiving institution rather than with the credential.",
        "costs": [
          "Some employers, licensing boards and military entry paths treat an equivalency differently from a diploma."
        ],
        "routes": [
          "Asking the licensing board, recruiter or admissions office directly, since each sets its own acceptance rule.",
          "Completing an adult diploma program, which yields the diploma itself where an equivalency will not be accepted.",
          "Career and technical certificate programs at community colleges, which stack toward a degree later."
        ],
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "never": {
        "tends": "Adults without a secondary credential work, raise families, own homes, run businesses and hold licenses. The credential functions as a filter at specific gates — many employer applications, most college admissions, some occupational licenses, enlistment — so its absence is felt at those gates and not continuously. Several skilled trades set their own entry requirements and admit on them.",
        "costs": [
          "A defined set of formal gates screens for the credential, and those gates do not reopen on their own."
        ],
        "routes": [
          "Registered apprenticeships, a number of which admit on their own entry requirements and pay during training.",
          "Occupational licensing routes that examine skill directly rather than schooling.",
          "The state equivalency test, which stays open at every adult age and has no expiry."
        ],
        "evidence": "speculative"
      }
    },
    "major": true,
    "affectsLater": [
      "ms-enrolling-in-college"
    ]
