    "analysis": {
      "early": {
        "tends": "Many states let registration be completed before the qualifying age is reached, so the paperwork is done and the registration activates on its own. Registration is address-bound, so one filed from an address that does not change soon after tends to need fewer later updates than one filed from an address still in flux. Schools, licensing offices and registration drives account for a large share of first registrations, because each is a moment when the paperwork is already open.",
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "window": {
        "tends": "Reaching the qualifying age makes a person eligible; it does not register them. In most states registration is a separate step with its own deadline weeks before an election, and a frequent reason an eligible person cannot vote in a given year is a registration that no longer matches a current address rather than a decision not to take part.",
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "late": {
        "tends": "Voting for the first time at an older age is ordinary, and what stands in the way is almost always administrative rather than motivational: the deadline, the address, the identification rule, and knowing which of them apply in a given state. Same-day registration in a number of states and online registration in most collapse several of those steps into one.",
        "costs": [
          "Registration deadlines in many states fall weeks before election day, so a late start can miss one cycle entirely."
        ],
        "routes": [
          "Same-day or election-day registration, offered in a number of states.",
          "Online voter registration through the state election office, available in most states.",
          "State election office lookup tools, which show registration status, deadlines and identification rules for one address."
        ],
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "interrupted": {
        "tends": "Moving is what breaks a registration. Because registration attaches to an address, a move across a county or state line usually requires a new one and sometimes new documents, so people who move often — students, renters, service members — have their voting interrupted far more by logistics than by opinion. List maintenance can also remove a registration that has gone unused, and that tends to be discovered at the polling place.",
        "costs": [
          "A registration can lapse quietly after a move or a stretch of years without voting, and the discovery often comes too late in a cycle to fix it.",
          "Identification and residency documents for a new address take time to assemble."
        ],
        "routes": [
          "Provisional ballots, which every state must offer where a registration cannot be confirmed at the polls.",
          "The federal write-in absentee ballot and the state department program for citizens abroad and for service members.",
          "Updating registration during a licensing agency transaction, which most states process under the federal voter registration law.",
          "State election office status lookups, which show a lapse before an election rather than on the day."
        ],
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "alternative": {
        "tends": "Federal contests are the least frequent items on most ballots. School boards, municipal offices, judicial retention and ballot measures decide much of what is locally binding and are settled by far smaller electorates. Public comment periods, local board seats, jury service and petitioning act on the same institutions by routes that do not run through a ballot at all.",
        "evidence": "speculative"
      },
      "never": {
        "tends": "Not voting is common and lawful, and eligibility does not expire: someone who has never cast a ballot is not thereby outside the process, and registration stays open at any later age with no penalty for the years unused. Civic weight also travels by other means — local participation, organizing, service and advocacy act on the same institutions.",
        "routes": [
          "Voter registration through a state election office, which remains open at any adult age.",
          "Local board, commission and public comment participation, which is open to residents regardless of registration.",
          "Community organizations and volunteer service, which act on the same institutions by other routes."
        ],
        "evidence": "speculative"
      }
    },
